---
description: >-
  Enrich an in-progress OCSF event with context from the syslog frame that
  carried it: hostname → logger.device.hostname, timestamp → logger.logged_time,
  app_name → logger.name.

  Call this after fortinet::fortigate::ocsf::map, while the fields that
  parse_syslog produced are still at the top level of the event and the OCSF
  record still lives in its own field. Calling it after the OCSF record has
  replaced `this` finds none of those fields.
args:
  named:
    - name: event
      description: The field that holds the in-progress OCSF event.
      type: field
---

// The syslog fields sit at the top level of the event, alongside rather than
// inside the OCSF record this writes to, so they are read unqualified.
//
// `timestamp` is when the relay stamped the frame. RFC 5424 states it in full
// and `parse_syslog` returns a time. The older BSD format gives only a month,
// day, and clock reading with no year, which arrives as a string; `time` turns
// that into null rather than inventing a year, and warns once per pipeline so
// the omission is visible. The event's own `time` is unaffected either way,
// since it comes from FortiGate's `eventtime` rather than from the frame.
$event.metadata.loggers = [{
  device: {
    hostname: hostname?,
  },
  logged_time: (timestamp? else null).time(),
  name: app_name?,
  log_format: "syslog",
}]
